  "narration": [
    {
      "t": 0.5,
      "text": "Here is the whole life of a drop: create it, fill it, close it. The order is not optional."
    },
    {
      "t": 7.09,
      "text": "The facts we have to record are sitting in a file, one per line, a name and then its value."
    },
    {
      "t": 13.28,
      "text": "If we go straight at it, zep stops us. There is nothing to stage into yet."
    },
    {
      "t": 18.71,
      "text": "So we create the drop first, and again zep gives us the nonce for it."
    },
    {
      "t": 23.56,
      "text": "Now the facts go in, one at a time, name first and then the value."
    },
    {
      "t": 28.67,
      "text": "Same again for the second one."
    },
    {
      "t": 32.3,
      "text": "And we close it, passing the nonce along, which is what actually seals it."
    },
    {
      "t": 37.8,
      "text": "Try to add anything now and zep refuses. That is why everything has to go in before you close it."
    },
    {
      "t": 44.28,
      "text": "Sealed, anchored, both facts in it."
    },
    {
      "t": 47.37,
      "text": "Create, fill, then close with the nonce. Once it is sealed, it is sealed."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Teaches that staging requires an existing drop, that the close needs the nonce, and that staging is refused after sealing."
}
